{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/439442-sell-me-on-mummy","id":439442,"title":"Sell me on mummy!","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-03-12T23:03:41-07:00","id":439442,"title":"Sell me on mummy!","content":"So mummy is the one game line I know nothing about, and is the only core book I don't own. I felt it was too limiting with character's all having been from an ancient civilization, and felt like it had little room for crossovers(which i always like to leave open). which of course I could be wrong about.<br /> <br /> So I'm curious. What are they about? what can they do out of the box?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1377-wrequis","name":"Wrequis","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-12T23:15:01-07:00","id":439485,"title":"","content":"For myself, some of those things (all the characters come from the same Ancient Civilisation, little attention paid to crossovers) actually <i>are</i> selling points. That said, my big ones for Mummy are the following:<br /> <br /> -It's a game about history. As history graduate, I like being able to throw my players into flashbacks in a variety of vivid and interesting periods of history.<br /> <br /> -It's a game about personal history. Players only get control over the barest smidgen of their 6,000 year backstories, and yet I've never seen players so invested and interested in their characters. They only have a rough idea of who they are now, and almost no idea over who they were. The zealousness with which some players pursue their own past never fails to amaze and please me.<br /> <br /> -It's a game about power versus individuality. Mummies, when they first wake, are incredibly potent creatures even without their powers, almost regardless of whether you built a combat character or not. The central crux of the game is about playing off that exploration of who you were and discovering your own identity with the struggle to hold onto your powers. You can sacrifice your own sense of self to try and cling to your power for as long as you can, or try to embrace your own individuality and sense of self at the cost of your gods-given power.<br /> <br /> I love that, as RPGs in general go, it's relatively unique in its basic premise. You don't get much control over who your character is, but you start with powers that would make an endgame character in many gamelines weep in terror. As the game progresses you can gradually lose these powers, but in return you get increasing control over your own history and personality. Or you can allow the centuries to grind your personality to dust in the pursuit of holding on to your fading glory.<br /> <br /> The game has often been equated to old age metaphors, and it really isn't a hard comparison to make.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1059-azahul","name":"Azahul","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1465"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-12T23:27:33-07:00","id":439496,"title":"","content":"Things I love about Mummy: <br /> <br /> The inverse relationship between power and memory/humanity/identity. So, at the start of a chronicle, you've got these crazy powerful protagonists, magically speaking, who have their own cults, ready to do their bidding. Mummies at Sekhem 10, with full access to their tier 3 Utterances, are on par with archmages, power-wise. <br /> <br /> And the protagonists also can't remember who they are. They're devoid of everything except the faint traces of memory and identity - and, of course, the Judges' commands. Do the benefits of crazy magic powers and a cult of willing servants outweigh the downsides of lacking a self? <br /> <br /> As time progresses, mummies slowly rebuild their memories - pity that they also get weaker, power-wise, doing so. Mummy is an analogy to old age - mummies live on borrowed time, and the mummy that's an unstoppable powerhouse in session 1 can be brought down by a single mortal in session 10. <br /> <br /> Exploring social themes through the perspective of immortal beings who're acutely aware of what has and what hasn't changed - this one's also really interesting. How does a person from the Stone Age feel about the prosperity of western societies, when contrasted with the faces of the poor in Lebanon or Kinshasa, or, heck, right at home?. Mummies feel at home when being with the poor and downtrodden and exploited - it's what they themselves are. Mummies have been created to serve and kill and die for beings of great power, and they have done so for 6000 years. <br /> <br /> Did I mention I really like the powers of Mummy? Utterances are very biblical in proportion and theme. Parting the Red Sea. Pulling a Babel Tower. Becoming a pillar of flame. Speaking through fire. That sort of thing. <br /> <br /> Sadikh - the ghoul analogy to mummies - are really great. So mummies are essentially eternally living servants of Egyptian gods - blessed/cursed to serve for all eternity. Their existence is chained to a god. <br /> <br /> Sadikh are there to provide the other side of the experience - a mummy may take a person who freely decides to serve the mummy and make him her unaging servant. So when they choose (intentionally) to accept an Arisen as their master, where they freely give away their true names, they chain their entire existence to the Arisen - they're only active and alive when their Arisen master is, experiencing something like a virtual reality false life while the Arisen is in Duat. A Sadikh cannot, under any circumstance, disobey his master while in his presence. They get to have powers, yes, but those are only borrowed from the Arisen, who can at any time choose not to let the Sadikh access them. The Sadikh can resurrect, but only if the Arisen allows it.<br /> <br /> The parallels to the Arisen and the Judges are obvious - and I find that sort of thing really interesting to explore. <br /> <br /> Lastly, might I recommend taking a look at the Mummy story hook thread? I think that it demonstrates the range of possible stories that Mummy supports quite nicely. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/286-christian-a","name":"Christian A","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"832"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-12T23:32:17-07:00","id":439500,"title":"","content":"Very interesting. Though I am curious as to who has the final say of who your character is? is it what you as the player choose to make your character do? Or what your story teller chooses what your character has done. <br /> <br /> In that i mean, yes, the players choose what they do if they are in control of a flashback, but the story teller places them in flashbacks which alludes that the characters chose to be at that place in time. no?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1377-wrequis","name":"Wrequis","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"111"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-12T23:42:16-07:00","id":439525,"title":"","content":"You are privy to secrets no man has known in thousands of years, the secrets of what rests in the dark of space and beyond the veil of death. You are the heir to the pinnacle of humanity, and grasp at the shadows created by the light of your fallen glory. You have the power of a god burning in your veins and the worship any mortal faith would kill for. <br /> <br /> To be less purple about it all, Mummy has a rich lore that feels heavy and downright <i>dense</i>, in the best possible way; read <i>Book of the Deceived </i>and marvel at how richly the mythology there is painted. It opens up the entire span of human history for gameplay, and allows you to jump between any time periods you could possibly want with no need for narrative excuses. The players get a hilarious power level, but get to see that the ability to instantly kill one of their peers doesn't mean much when you're all Deathless. Rather than play in the corners of the World of Darkness, you get to be one of the movers and shakers, the <i>things</i> at the heart of international conspiracies. <br /> <br /> And if you're a crossover fan, there's plenty there! Vampire is a game that similarly has excuses to explore all of history, and any elder/methuselah could pose an interesting rival to one of the Arisen. <i>Ancient Mysteries</i> demonstrates the state of Kindred in Ancient Egypt, which is definitely a fun sandbox for Mummy. Werewolf tosses in the Sumerian city-state of Bau (ruled by werewolf god-kings and described in the <i>Forsaken Chronicler's Guide</i>), which could be a downright fascinating plot tie-in for a Mummy chronicle. Mage has the superficially similar stories of Atlantis and Irem, and Mummy is popping up in two Mage chapters of <i>Dark Eras</i>. Promethean could very well be hiding a tie between the Nepri lineage and the Arisen. Hunter has the Ascending Ones (who have every excuse to connect to Mummy) and Aegis Kai Doru (perhaps even moreso), plus the two Mummy-specific groups in <i>Mortal Remains</i>. <br /> <br /> I'm speaking as someone who <b><i>hated </i></b>Mummy from its Kickstarter until <i>Book of the Deceived </i>dropped, but that book's quality sold me on the rest of the line and now it's become my favorite nWoD game. It's worth a shot, doubly so if you like history at all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Remi. she/her. game designer.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/281-atamajakki","name":"atamajakki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar281_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5487"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-12T23:42:36-07:00","id":439526,"title":"","content":"Players have control of who their characters are NOW.<br /> The Storyteller can influence who those characters WERE.<br /> <br /> The simplest way I can describe it is that the PC's are Douglas Quaid from Total Recall or Jason Bourne from the Bourne Identity, with slightly more control over their back-story.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-13T00:16:19-07:00","id":439543,"title":"","content":"To kinda answer your question <b>Wrequies</b>, yes the ST is in control over what your character has done in the grand scheme of things and no they don't have the final say over who your character is. (Curses! <b>FallenEco</b> beat me to the punch...)<br /> <br /> Because the Deathless cannot percieve the full extent of their existance without a great deal of work and sacrifice a Mummy will only ever remember the past two Descents or so during a normal Chronicle. In moments when their past does come to the surface they may find that the person they were in the past is quite different than the person they are now and it's up to the players to piece together what could have changed. Not only is the game filled with a myriad of clues to help players with this and St's to concoct great stories but the game encourages players to be creative with their characters by crafting their name, concept, Decree, Judge/Temkhat (Won't go into that now though), states, powers, and merits to all reflect not only who they are now but who they were during their previous Descents. And all of that is chosen by the player, it's just up to the ST to do somthing interesting with it.<br /> <br /> There is a certain level of trust that is put upon the ST by the players in a Mummy game becauce they are handing over control of their character to another person and that's not an easy idea to get behind, but if you can then it highlights the greatest aspect of Mummy: the Curse. And that's it's deep seeded sense of disempowerment.<br /> <br /> I can show you an example of how this could be done with just the bare minimum of information, if you're planing on running Mummy then this could be a beta test for crafting flashbacks for your own Chronicle. If you'd like to be a player instead then I know <b>Azahul</b> can cook up a good one for this character.<br /> <br /> Five things: Name, Concept, Decree, Judge, and Guild. Craft me a flashback for this Arisen.<br /> <br /> Name: Faal Ure-Rajek, the Smiling Shadow<br /> Concept: Scourge of the dissident and keeper of kingdoms.<br /> Decree: Jackal-Headed<br /> Judge: Tenemhu, the Retreater<br /> Guild: Maa-Kep<br /> <br /> Hope this helps. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3436,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","user":"Dusksage","date":"03-13-2015, 03:19 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-13T00:33:53-07:00","id":439557,"title":"","content":"The game isn't less crossover-friendly than any of the other games. <b>Werewolf</b>, <b>Mage</b>, and <b>Geist</b> exist as an exception because of sharing the spirit and ghostly realms, but rarely is there crossover mentioned except in supplements - of which <b>Mummy</b> only has a couple out right now anyway.<br /> <br /> Even in the core book alone there's a slew of affinities and utterances that are designed with other supernatural powers and situations in mind","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/638-reseru","name":"reseru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar638_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1574"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-03-13T07:50:09-07:00","id":439910,"title":"","content":"I'd argue that cults are a great vehicle for crossover:<br /> For Mages, cults provide a vast source of Mysteries all wrapped up in ancient ruins.<br /> For solitary Werewolves, cults provide a surrogate pack and nasty things to hunt.<br /> For old Vampires, the Arisen are eternal companions (who may not remember them).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: the Ascension - <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1191028-redesigned-prime-sphere\" target=\"_blank\">Redesigned Prime Sphere</a>; <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1188769-simpler-wonder-creation\" target=\"_blank\">Streamlined Wonder Creation</a><br /> Mummy: the Curse - <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/474927-mummy-the-curse-2e-conversion\" target=\"_blank\">Lightweight 2E Conversion</a>; <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/22378-second-sight-tradition-disciples-of-duat\" target=\"_blank\">Disciples of Duat</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/61-octavo","name":"Octavo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar61_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1171"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}